WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Similar-looking curves can describe different mathematical objects. Start with their definitions.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Take a line in the input plane. Apply the point map w = z².

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Matching dots show z = 1 + iy and w = (1 − y²) + i(2y).

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Now choose V = Re(z²) = x² − y² as a local potential. That is a modelling choice.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Dashed contours keep V constant. Solid field lines follow E = −grad V, crossing regular contours normally.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:48.000
These ideal field lines follow B in three dimensions, outside the excluded source. This is a different model.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
The same two fixed 3D curves have different depths. In this x,z view, their outlines overlap.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:04.000
The orthographic camera turns by 30°. All physical points and the magnetic field stay fixed.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Their projections separate. The dashed curve is edge-on; the underlying curve has not collapsed.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Which object has a tangent parallel to its vector field: mapped grid, potential contour, or field line?

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:26.000
The answer is field line. Definitions and equations establish the connection; visual resemblance alone does not.
